  "resolution_criteria": "This question will resolve as the \"Total Public Debt Outstanding\" on December 31, 2026, according to the official [Debt to the Penny dataset](https://fiscaldata.treasury.gov/datasets/debt-to-the-penny/debt-to-the-penny).",
  "fine_print": "",
  "background": "The [national debt of the United States](https://fiscaldata.treasury.gov/datasets/debt-to-the-penny/debt-to-the-penny#introduction) is the debt, or unpaid borrowed funds, carried by the federal government of the United States, which is measured by intra-governmental holdings and debt held by the public, including securities issued by the U.S. Treasury. The U.S. national debt has been [climbing at a rapid pace](https://www.foxbusiness.com/economy/us-national-debt-tracker) for years.\n\nAt the end of 2025, [the national debt](https://fiscaldata.treasury.gov/datasets/debt-to-the-penny/debt-to-the-penny#introduction) stood at just over \\$38.5 trillion. By mid-August 2026, the figure had reached [over \\$40 trillion](https://apnews.com/article/treasury-national-debt-limit-a27a8d3651ff810b25c610d3e1b6259d), equivalent to about a 4% increase, and over \\$6 billion per day during 2026.",
